    {
      "title": "Shark trapped for a month in South Korean canal is finally freed",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/21/youtube-watch-over-shark-stuck-south-korea-canal-busan-bukang-i",
      "description": "<p>Bukang-i successfully guided out after month in which it drew 1.6m visitors and was watched over by YouTubers</p><p>In <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/south-korea\">South Korea</a>, they call them <em>jagyeongdan</em>, a word whose meaning sits somewhere between vigilante group and neighbourhood watch. Each night they put on hi-vis vests, picked up their selfie sticks and gathered around the harbour in Busan to keep watch over an unlikely charge: a <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/sep/29/large-shark-stuck-city-canal-south-korea-rescue\">stranded shark</a> affectionately known as Bukang-i.</p><p>On Wednesday, after more than a month in the canal and at the third attempt, the shark was successfully guided out, South Korea’s oceans ministry said. Officials expect her to move into open water in time.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/21/youtube-watch-over-shark-stuck-south-korea-canal-busan-bukang-i\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-21T11:41:48"
    },
    {
      "title": "Shark Returns to Sea After Over a Month Stuck in a South Korean Port Canal",
